        <tbody>
          {shock_rows_html}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col" style="margin-top:24px;">
      <div class="insight-box red">
        <strong>Largest Single-Month Gain:</strong> +217.07% in January 1974 — the post-embargo OPEC price reset from $4.10 to $13.00 in one step. This remains the single most extreme monthly percentage move in the dataset.
      </div>
      <div class="insight-box red">
        <strong>Largest Absolute Monthly Drop:</strong> −$26.96/bbl in October 2008 — the GFC demand collapse. From $72.69 to $53.97, erasing over a year of gains in a single month. The absolute magnitude dwarfs all pre-2000s shocks.
      </div>
      <div class="insight-box">
        <strong>COVID-19 Shock Sequence:</strong> March 2020 saw −39.63% ($61.63 → $32.20). April 2020 bottomed at $21.04 — the dataset's second-lowest level since 1999. The recovery was equally sharp: +44.37% by May 2020 on OPEC+ cuts.
      </div>
      <div class="insight-box blue">
        <strong>Volatility Regimes:</strong> Rolling σ analysis reveals three high-turbulence periods: 1973–1981 (embargo/revolution), 2007–2009 (GFC cycle), and 2014–2016 (shale price war). The 1990s stands out as the lowest-volatility regime post-1974.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===== SECTION 5: STATISTICAL SUMMARY ===== -->
<div class="section" id="statistical">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-id">05 · Statistical Summary & Distribution</div>
      <h2>Descriptive Statistics & Price Distribution</h2>
      <p>Full distributional analysis of the 675-observation dataset. The right-skewed distribution and mean/median divergence reflect the dataset's extreme high-price episodes in the 2000s and early 2010s.</p>
