<!-- ===== SECTION 6: OUTLOOK ===== -->
<div class="section" id="outlook">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-id">06 · Outlook & Conclusions</div>
      <h2>Recent Trajectory and Analytical Conclusions</h2>
      <p>Synthesizing the 2020s data visible in the dataset alongside long-term structural patterns to form evidence-based conclusions about where crude oil pricing stands within its historical context.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-title">2020–2026 Price Detail</div>
      <div class="chart-subtitle">Monthly prices for the most recent period, showing COVID crash, recovery, Ukraine war spike, and gradual normalization</div>
      <div class="chart-wrap" style="height:280px;">
        <canvas id="chartRecent"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="three-col" style="margin-top:24px;">
      <div class="insight-box">
        <strong>2020 COVID Shock:</strong> Prices fell from $61.63 (Jan 2020) to $21.04 (Apr 2020) — a 65.9% collapse in 3 months, the fastest absolute decline since 2008. The $21.04 April floor was the lowest monthly price since November 1999 ($24.16). Recovery was driven by OPEC+ production cuts and vaccine-driven demand revival.
      </div>
      <div class="insight-box">
        <strong>2021–22 Recovery:</strong> Prices recovered steadily to $72.86 by Dec 2021, then accelerated sharply on the Russia-Ukraine war: March 2022 hit $112.40, and June 2022 reached $116.80 — the highest since October 2012. The speed of the 2022 rally (+36% in H1 2022) surpassed even the 2009 recovery.
      </div>
      <div class="insight-box blue">
        <strong>2023–25 Normalization:</strong> Prices have trended from ~$80 in Jan 2023 to ~$60–68 by late 2025. This gravitational pull toward the $60–80 range likely reflects OPEC+ supply management countering weak Chinese demand and a persistent shale output floor in the US. The 2025 average of $63.81/bbl is the lowest since 2021.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-box" style="margin-top:8px;">
      <strong>2026 Data Note:</strong> The dataset contains three 2026 data points (Jan: $60.04, Feb: $64.47, Mar: $87.84). The March 2026 figure of $87.84 represents a +36.25% jump from February — the fourth-largest single-month percentage gain in the entire 56-year dataset. Per the dataset documentation, 2026 values may be projections rather than observed actuals. Analysts should treat this spike with appropriate caution until confirmed by market data.
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:32px; background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; padding:28px;">
      <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:16px; color:#fff;">Key Analytical Conclusions</h3>
      <div style="display:grid; gap:12px;">
        <div style="display:flex; gap:12px; align-items:flex-start;">
          <div style="background:var(--accent); color:#000; font-size:11px; font-weight:700; padding:2px 8px; border-radius:4px; white-space:nowrap; margin-top:2px;">01</div>
          <div style="font-size:0.9rem; color:var(--text);"><strong style="color:var(--accent);">No return to pre-1973 levels.</strong> The 1973 embargo established a permanent structural floor. The dataset's lowest post-1974 price was $9.62/bbl (Jul 1986), and even the COVID-19 demand shock only pushed prices to $21/bbl — 17× above the 1970 level.</div>
        </div>
        <div style="display:flex; gap:12px; align-items:flex-start;">
          <div style="background:var(--accent2); color:#000; font-size:11px; font-weight:700; padding:2px 8px; border-radius:4px; white-space:nowrap; margin-top:2px;">02</div>
          <div style="font-size:0.9rem; color:var(--text);"><strong style="color:var(--accent2);">The 2000s supercycle was an anomaly of scale, not kind.</strong> Price cycles existed in every decade, but the 2000s saw an unprecedented convergence of Chinese demand growth, financial instrument proliferation, and constrained OPEC spare capacity — producing moves 5–10× larger in absolute terms than any prior cycle.</div>
        </div>
        <div style="display:flex; gap:12px; align-items:flex-start;">
          <div style="background:var(--green); color:#000; font-size:11px; font-weight:700; padding:2px 8px; border-radius:4px; white-space:nowrap; margin-top:2px;">03</div>
          <div style="font-size:0.9rem; color:var(--text);"><strong style="color:var(--green);">The shale revolution created a durable $50–80 range floor and ceiling.</strong> Post-2014 data consistently shows gravitational pulls back to this band. Both the COVID overshoot downward and the Ukraine war overshoot upward reverted to this zone within 12–18 months.</div>
        </div>
        <div style="display:flex; gap:12px; align-items:flex-start;">
          <div style="background:var(--purple); color:#000; font-size:11px; font-weight:700; padding:2px 8px; border-radius:4px; white-space:nowrap; margin-top:2px;">04</div>
          <div style="font-size:0.9rem; color:var(--text);"><strong style="color:var(--purple);">Volatility is regime-dependent, not random.</strong> Rolling σ analysis shows clear high-turbulence regimes (1973–81, 2007–09, 2014–16) separated by relative calm. Analysts should model crude oil as a regime-switching process rather than assuming constant volatility.</div>
        </div>
